<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Himyarite Kingdom</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Himyarite</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Himyarite Kingdom was a state in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, that dominated the region in late antiquity. Its rulers adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> in the late period. In the sixth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire</a> intervened militarily in Himyar after the persecution of Christians there, bringing it temporarily under Aksumite control.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/himyarite-kingdom.jpg" alt="Himyarite Kingdom" width="320" height="231" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DamiKarv · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Himyarite_Kingdom.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Himyar's original heartland lay in the southern highlands of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>. Until around 110 BCE it formed part of the Qatabanian kingdom, after which it emerged as an independent state with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zafar-yemen">Ẓafār</a>. Its rulers were associated with dhū-Raydān, and they were first styled kings of Sabaʾ and dhū-Raydān. Himyar conquered Sabaʾ for the first time in about 25 BCE, Qataban in about 200 CE and Ḥaḍramawt around 300 CE. By the late third century it controlled the Sabaean kingdom for good and ruled most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabia</a>, uniting the region for the first time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The kingdom was a confederation of tribes and a hub of trade linking the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-east">Middle East</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Yemen supplied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> with frankincense and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/myrrh">myrrh</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliny-the-elder">Pliny the Elder</a> called it one of the richest nations in the world. Himyarite ships regularly travelled along the East African coast. From the mid-fourth century inscriptions record expeditions into central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, and by about 440 Himyar had annexed Maʿadd and placed garrisons there. It kept extensive control of the interior for nearly two centuries.</p><p>From the mid- to late fourth century the ruling class adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> or a Jewish-inflected monotheism, and inscriptions stopped naming polytheistic gods and instead invoked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rahmanan">Rahmanan</a>, the Lord of Heaven and Earth, and sometimes the God of Israel. Sources credit the change to Malkīkarib Yuha'min, who reigned about 375–400, while traditional Islamic sources place it under his son Abu Karib. Native Christian kings ruled from 500 to 521–522, and an Aksumite invasion brought Christian rule in the sixth century. Himyar then had an Aksumite-backed regime. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abraha">Abraha</a> later sponsored churches and a restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marib-dam">Marib Dam</a>, as an inscription of 548 records. Sasanian intervention brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> under Sasanian influence in 575 or 578.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Himyarite families outlived the kingdom. The aristocratic Dhu'l-Kala and Dhu Asbah led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabian</a> contingents in the Muslim conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homs">Homs</a> in 638 and made it a centre of South Arabian settlement and political power. Their chiefs supported Mu'awiya ibn Abi Sufyan against Caliph Ali, and their influence waned after the Battle of Marj Rahit in 684 and the death of their leader at the Battle of Khazir in 686. Medieval Muslim genealogists placed Himyar among the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> descended from Qahtan, and after the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, Himyarite noble families re-established control over parts of the country.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Himyar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At the same time new powers rose in the highlands, above all the <b>Himyarites</b> of Zafar, who formed a rival kingdom in the late second century BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-saba">Kingdom of Saba · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
